  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting for the Vocational and Socio-Economic Integration of Young Guineans to Mitigate the Risks Associated with Irregular Migration</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Youth constitute most of the Guinean population with an average age of 22 years. Many young people have not accessed the education system and those who have accessed who have shortened the normal school curriculum are directed to technical and vocational training courses and centers. One of the reasons for the high youth unemployment rate is mainly due to the inadequacy of training in relation to the needs of the job market and limited access to financing. 
 
Guinea is considered a country with a high rate of departure of young people who choose irregular and dangerous paths that put them in a vulnerable situation. To meet the challenge of the socio-professional and economic integration of students leaving the CFP and to combat irregular migration, the Guinean State has set up a ten-year education program (ProDeG 2019-2029) with the aim of initiating reforms to adapt training to the needs of the economy and also to respond to the challenges of quality and relevance of teaching through apprenticeship in the Vocational Training Centers (CFP), National School of Agriculture and Livestock (ENAE), Regional Schools of Arts and Crafts (ERAM). 
 
The implementation of the youth self-employment project and the one on vocational training financed by the SEM has made it possible to identify very good results and good practices during management. The continuity of support for young people through self-employment will be the continuation of previous projects and intends to provide a short-term response to the integration needs of young people through support for qualifying training and entrepreneurship.</narrative>
  </description>
